            <ArticleTitle>Intrathoracic gastric volvulus mimicking pyloric stenosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>149-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 5-week-old-infant presented to hospital following the acute onset of non-bilious vomiting with clinical and acid-base features suggestive of pyloric stenosis. A chest radiograph obtained because of intercurrent infection unexpectedly revealed a left-sided congenital diaphragmatic hernia. A barium meal demonstrated the presence of an intrathoracic gastric volvulus, requiring urgent surgical management. We discuss the presentation and management of this rare surgical cause of non-bilious vomiting in infancy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
